by lyrixx at 2026-09-22T07:34:35Z

I pushed a reworked version, taking into account the reviews here and the discussion around #66185.

```php
#[AsWorkflow(supports: Task::class)]
final class TaskWorkflow
{
    use WorkflowTrait;

    #[Transition(from: TaskStep::New, to: TaskStep::Processing)]
    public const START = 'start';

    #[AsGuardListener(transition: self::START)]
    public function guardStart(GuardEvent $event): void { /* ... */ }
}
```

* The definition lives on a regular class with `#[AsWorkflow]`. Transitions are declared with `#[Transition]` on constants (the constant value is the name, the attribute is repeatable) or in a `transitions:` list. Places are inferred from the transitions; `#[Place]` metadata are read from the string-backed enums used in the definition. Everything the `workflow` configuration offers is available, and a minimal definition only needs the transitions.
* The class stays an ordinary service with a free constructor. The opt-in `WorkflowTrait` exposes the methods of `Workflow` (a reflection test keeps the signatures in sync), so custom methods like `startProcess()` are straightforward.
* The `workflow` argument of the `As*Listener` attributes can be omitted inside a class using `#[AsWorkflow]`.
* YAML and attributes now feed the same `@internal` descriptor and registrar, inside the `WorkflowBundle`. The parameter that crossed the package boundary is gone.
* The remarks of @stof (repeatable transitions, `from`/`froms`, `name` on constants, array shapes) and @alexandre-daubois (weights, audit trail default, trait detection, name collisions, disabled bundle, `getMarking()` context, error messages) are addressed, with tests at the component level plus a FrameworkBundle functional test.

